If Earth stood still, wind would blow straight from high to low pressure. But [[@what-if-earth-stopped|Earth spins]]. As air moves over the spinning planet, its path seems to curve: to the right in the northern [[hemisphere|hemisphere]] and to the left in the southern. This is called the [[@wind-patterns|Coriolis effect]].

It is why big storms spin. [[@hurricanes|Cyclones]] over the Bay of Bengal and [[hurricane|hurricanes]] over the Atlantic turn anticlockwise, while those south of the [[equator|equator]] turn clockwise. It also shapes the steady trade winds that [[@boats-sail|sailing ships]] once relied on to cross oceans.

{{story:kite-flying}}

In 1832, while sailing off South America, Charles Darwin noticed tiny spiders landing on his ship, the Beagle, far from land. They had travelled through the air on silk.

Young spiders climb to a high point, raise their bodies and let out fine threads. Then they lift off. Scientists long assumed the wind did all the work. But in 2018, researchers in Britain showed that spiders also react to Earth's natural electric field, which may help pull their charged silk upward.

{{story:spiders-silk}}

Sea snakes breathe with lungs, so every so often they have to swim up for air. One lung runs almost the whole length of the body and works like an air tank. Some species can also absorb a little oxygen straight through their skin.

With these tricks, some sea snakes stay underwater for an hour or more, and a few go much longer while resting. Compare that with your own [[@lungs-breathe|lungs]]: most people can hold their breath for less than a minute. Divers watching sea snakes see them rise slowly, snatch a quick breath at the surface and glide back down, like a tiny whale coming up to blow.

{{story:lungs-breathe}}

A platypus dives for about 30 to 140 seconds at a time, hunting worms, shrimp and insect [[larva|larvae]] on riverbeds. Underwater it shuts its eyes, ears and nostrils with folds of skin. It is basically diving blind. It also can't hold much food in its mouth, so it stuffs prey into cheek pouches and eats at the surface, floating on its belly like a fluffy cushion. A platypus can eat around a fifth of its own body weight in a single night.

Instead, it sweeps its bill from side to side. About 40,000 [[sensor|sensors]] in the soft skin pick up the weak electricity made by a moving animal's muscles. Tens of thousands more sense touch and ripples. An [[@power-electric-eel|electric eel]] makes electricity to hunt. The platypus reads it.

{{story:australia}}

Traditionally, phad painters made their own colours from minerals and plants. Yellow from a stone, red from earth or other minerals, green from copper-based colours, and black from lamp soot. Colours were ground into powder and mixed with plant gum and water.

The cloth is hand-woven cotton, soaked in rice or wheat [[starch|starch]] and polished smooth with a stone, so the paint doesn't soak in. Each [[pigment|pigment]] is applied in a fixed order, one colour across the whole cloth before moving to the next. The bold black outlines usually go on at the very end.

{{story:storytelling-india}}

Children have always helped on farms. But during the [[@industrial-revolution|Industrial Revolution]], in the 1700s and 1800s, they were sent into factories and mines in large numbers. They were cheap to hire and small enough to crawl under machines. Some worked 12 hours a day.

The writer Charles Dickens was sent to work in a shoe-polish factory at the age of 12, when his father was jailed for debt. He never forgot it, and poor, overworked children appear all through his novels.

{{story:charles-dickens}}

Here is how a talk with Socrates might go. Someone says courage means never running away. Socrates asks whether a soldier who retreats to trick the enemy and then wins is a coward. The person admits he is not, and tries again. Socrates asks more questions, and the definition keeps breaking.

This back-and-forth is now called the Socratic method. Socrates said he was like his mother, a midwife: he didn't give people ideas, but helped them give birth to their own. Teachers and law schools still use the method today, and it is a big part of what [[@philosophy-kids|philosophy]] means.

{{story:democracy-athens}}

A famous legend says a clever inventor asked a king for 1 grain of rice on the first square, 2 on the second, 4 on the third, [[@exponents|doubling every time]]. The king laughed and agreed. But doubling grows terrifyingly fast: the last square alone needs over 9 quintillion grains.

Chess itself has the same explosive maths. After just three moves each, there are millions of possible positions. In 1950, the American engineer Claude Shannon estimated that the number of possible games is a 1 followed by about 120 zeros. [[atom|Atoms]] in the observable universe come to roughly a 1 followed by 80 zeros.

{{story:board-games}}

For thousands of years, people in many cultures chewed willow bark or drank tea made from it to ease pain and fever. In the 1800s, chemists found the active substance, salicin. In 1897, a chemist at the German company Bayer, Felix Hoffmann, made a gentler version, acetylsalicylic [[acid|acid]]. Bayer sold it as aspirin from 1899.

For decades, nobody knew how it worked. In 1971, the British scientist John Vane showed that aspirin blocks an enzyme the body uses to make chemicals that cause pain, swelling and fever. He shared the 1982 Nobel Prize. Many medicines, from [[@antibiotics|penicillin]] to some heart drugs, began with substances found in nature.

{{story:inventions-accidents}}

A [[@microwave|microwave oven]] uses radio waves at about 2.45 gigahertz, very close to Wi-Fi's 2.4. In the oven, those waves are very strong and trapped inside a metal box, where they make water [[molecule|molecules]] in food jiggle and heat up.

A little of that energy can leak out, which is why some people notice their Wi-Fi slowing down when the microwave is on. Wi-Fi itself is far weaker, thousands of times less powerful than an oven, and health agencies have found no evidence that normal Wi-Fi harms people.

{{story:radio-waves}}

Many Goans wanted freedom too. Tristão de Bragança Cunha, an engineer and writer often called the father of Goan nationalism, founded the Goa Congress Committee in 1928.

On 18 June 1946, the socialist leader Ram Manohar Lohia spoke at a public meeting in Margao in defiance of a ban, and was arrested. That date is still marked as Goa Revolution Day. The Portuguese jailed many freedom fighters, and some, such as Tristão de Bragança Cunha, were sent to prison in Portugal itself.

{{story:goan-food}}

Base camp sits on the Khumbu [[@glaciers|Glacier]], a slow river of ice covered in rocks. Because the ice moves, tent platforms have to be rebuilt every season. At night, climbers hear the ice creak and the rumble of distant avalanches.

[[expedition|Expeditions]] arrive in April and stay for six weeks or more. Cooks prepare dal bhat, noodles, eggs and soup in big tents. Water comes from melted snow and ice. Before climbing, many teams hold a puja, a ceremony led by a Buddhist lama, asking the mountain for safe passage. Climbers and Sherpas throw rice, hang prayer flags and share sweets.

{{story:bar-headed-goose}}

Until 1972 the island was called Ceylon, and the name still lives on in its tea. [[@coffee|Coffee]] was the main crop under British rule until a plant disease wiped it out in the 1870s. Planters switched to tea. A Scottish planter called James Taylor had already started the first commercial tea estate in 1867, at Loolecondera near Kandy.

Today Sri Lanka is one of the world's biggest tea exporters. Tea pluckers, many of them Tamil women whose families came from southern India in the 1800s, pick the top two leaves and a bud by hand. The cool hill country around Nuwara Eliya is sometimes called "Little England" for its cool weather and old [[colonial|colonial]] bungalows. You can trace the drink's long journey in [[@tea|tea]].

{{story:tea}}

The book opens with the Mul Mantar, a short statement of belief that begins *Ik Onkar*: there is one God. Its hymns talk about remembering God, working honestly, sharing with others and seeing every person as equal, whatever their caste, religion or whether they are a man or a woman.

Most of the book is arranged by [[raga|raga]], the musical mode in which a hymn is sung, with about 31 ragas in all. Many hymns name which raga to use, and some ragas are traditionally sung at particular [[@raga-time|times of day]]. The language is mostly old Punjabi, mixed with Braj, Persian and other languages spoken across north India at the time.

{{story:raga-time}}

Narayan wrote in simple, gentle English about schoolboys, shopkeepers and astrologers. *Swami and Friends* (1935) follows a ten-year-old who hates arithmetic. Its stories later became the TV series *[[@malgudi|Malgudi Days]]*.

Ruskin Bond was born in Kasauli in 1934 and has lived in the hills near Mussoorie since the 1960s. He has written hundreds of stories, many for children, about trains, tigers and rain. His first novel, *The Room on the Roof*, was written when he was a teenager.

{{story:english-spread}}
